{"chapter": "morphisms", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "0H1L", "placeholders": {"STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:0d80cbe98c7a0f8e21cec9f693dcce01e16ad39b33cd108abdf1628d52bb4ccf", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nResults which do not fit elsewhere.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:fa4e546659067a1752d429834b7f1e74bfea5415a717e10b1bd1a59debbe6d6c", "unit_id": "tag:0H1L:prose-0001"}
{"chapter": "morphisms", "node_kind": "lemma", "parent_tag": "0H1L", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$f : Y \\to X$", "MATH_0002": "$x \\in X$", "MATH_0003": "$Y$", "MATH_0004": "$f(Y)$", "MATH_0005": "$\\{x\\}$", "MATH_0006": "$f$", "MATH_0007": "$x = \\Spec(\\kappa(x)) \\to X$"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{lemma}\n\\label{lemma-factor-through-point}\n", "suffix": "\\end{lemma}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:1501aeacceef20ef8a247f79a4f735ce6dfd1f73060cf8ee26afe264e8ea892f", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:28eb182f2d755012fec150a7b1a3285b8cbca18c6633f2e1ec162b4093fa4ec9", "source_text": "Let <MATH_0001> be a morphism of schemes. Let <MATH_0002> be a point.\nAssume that <MATH_0003> is reduced and <MATH_0004> is set-theoretically contained\nin <MATH_0005>. Then <MATH_0006> factors through the canonical morphism\n<MATH_0007>.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:f800dba4c9412d75148ab1ce6474716efad92ff3155dc9a5282fb0473648c0cd", "unit_id": "tag:0H1M:statement"}
{"chapter": "morphisms", "node_kind": "proof", "parent_tag": "0H1L", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$A \\to B$", "MATH_0002": "$B$", "MATH_0003": "$\\mathfrak p \\subset A$", "MATH_0004": "$\\Spec(B) \\to \\Spec(A)$", "MATH_0005": "$A \\to B$", "MATH_0006": "$\\kappa(\\mathfrak p)$"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{proof}\n", "suffix": "\\end{proof}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:fbfe4525be6cf73286fdfbcb320bbda8a434f77144a61c96bdadf2a9d2b091ae", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:9d6b7ce387be27f062eecccd03cf5ff3da53a3d18b2335f8584f72b00138a8d1", "source_text": "Omitted. Hints: working affine locally one reduces to a commutative algebra\nlemma. Given a ring map <MATH_0001> with <MATH_0002> reduced such that there exists\na unique prime ideal <MATH_0003> in the image of\n<MATH_0004>, then <MATH_0005> factors through <MATH_0006>.\nThis is a nice exercise.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:6c542b679ae37206ac2f3dc94f322c0a965b5928108215150b83f588666e7bf3", "unit_id": "tag:0H1M:proof"}
{"chapter": "morphisms", "node_kind": "lemma", "parent_tag": "0H1L", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$f : Y \\to X$", "MATH_0002": "$E \\subset X$", "MATH_0003": "$X$", "MATH_0004": "$E$", "MATH_0005": "$Y$", "MATH_0006": "$f(Y) \\subset E$", "MATH_0007": "$f$", "MATH_0008": "$\\coprod_{x \\in E} x \\to X$"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{lemma}\n\\label{lemma-factor-through-set-of-points}\n", "suffix": "\\end{lemma}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:a55043a7f42f87a849d4ef2e878154c260a45f1eec4963664b1eae519ee7b37c", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:45c25640481365f030e5cdf1181c174948ff8f220981d79dec2d0d2d2e72680b", "source_text": "Let <MATH_0001> be a morphism of schemes. Let <MATH_0002>.\nAssume <MATH_0003> is locally Noetherian, there are no nontrivial specializations\namong the elements of <MATH_0004>, <MATH_0005> is reduced, and <MATH_0006>.\nThen <MATH_0007> factors through <MATH_0008>.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:fb3b6542df24472b9c60aeeed1c705f6fceb23e15783e694f84857a536e1a8f6", "unit_id": "tag:0H1N:statement"}
{"chapter": "morphisms", "node_kind": "proof", "parent_tag": "0H1L", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$E$", "MATH_0002": "$E$", "MATH_0003": "$E$", "MATH_0004": "$X$", "MATH_0005": "$X$", "MATH_0006": "$Y$", "MATH_0007": "$f : Y \\to X$", "MATH_0008": "$\\varphi : A \\to B$", "MATH_0009": "$A' \\subset B$", "MATH_0010": "$\\varphi$", "MATH_0011": "$E' \\subset \\Spec(A') \\subset \\Spec(A)$", "MATH_0012": "$A'$", "MATH_0013": "$E'$", "MATH_0014": "$\\Spec(B) \\to \\Spec(A') \\subset \\Spec(A)$", "MATH_0015": "$E' \\subset E$", "MATH_0016": "$A'$", "MATH_0017": "$E'$", "MATH_0018": "$\\Spec(B) \\to \\Spec(A)$", "MATH_0019": "$E'$", "MATH_0020": "$E$", "MATH_0021": "$E'$", "MATH_0022": "$E$", "MATH_0023": "$E$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{lemma-factor-through-point}", "REF_0002": "\\ref{algebra-lemma-injective-minimal-primes-in-image}", "REF_0003": "\\ref{algebra-lemma-Noetherian-irreducible-components}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{proof}\n", "suffix": "\\end{proof}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:4f0818931083e3188dc8530712771911fa997425f7bb9a867834b8ce5da2b03b", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:848341d919d8b8e0bed2a32dd7dbb6124d0f70a02f0c35781cc36592d74c30ef", "source_text": "When <MATH_0001> is a singleton this follows from\nLemma <REF_0001>. If <MATH_0002> is finite, then <MATH_0003>\n(with the induced topology of <MATH_0004>) is a finite discrete space by\nour assumption on specializations. Hence this case reduces to the\nsingleton case. In general, there is a reduction to the\ncase where <MATH_0005> and <MATH_0006> are affine schemes. Say <MATH_0007> corresponds to\nthe ring map <MATH_0008>. Denote <MATH_0009> the image of <MATH_0010>.\nLet <MATH_0011> be the set of minimal primes\nof <MATH_0012>. By Algebra, Lemma <REF_0002>\nthe set <MATH_0013> is contained in the image of\n<MATH_0014>. We conclude that <MATH_0015>.\nSince <MATH_0016> is Noetherian we have <MATH_0017> is finite by Algebra, Lemma\n<REF_0003>.\nSince any other point in the image of <MATH_0018> is\na specialization of an element of <MATH_0019> and in <MATH_0020>, we conclude that\nthe image is contained in <MATH_0021> (by our assumption on specializations between\npoints of <MATH_0022>). Thus we reduce to the case where\n<MATH_0023> is finite which we dealt with above.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:f00f89b797813f6f3eb66da34d29dedc6cc18da778acbe7b2b106982f0a076bd", "unit_id": "tag:0H1N:proof"}
